        "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 42
      },
      "content": "Here you go:\n\n- **Virtual memory gives each process its own private, contiguous address space**, even though physical RAM is shared and fragmented. The OS maps virtual addresses to physical ones via **page tables**, so programs don't need to know where data actually lives.\n- **Memory is divided into fixed-size blocks called pages** (virtual) and **frames** (physical), typically 4 KB. Paging lets the OS load only the pages a program is actively using, keeping the rest on disk.\n- **A page fault happens when a program accesses a page not currently in RAM.** The CPU traps to the OS, which loads the page from disk into a free frame, updates the page table, and resumes the instruction — the program never notices.\n- **The TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer) is a small, fast cache of recent virtual-to-physical translations.** It avoids walking the page table on every memory access; a TLB miss falls back to the page table (or a page fault if the page isn't resident).\n- **The payoff:** processes get isolation and more address space than physical RAM, while the OS + hardware (MMU, TLB) handle translation and swapping transparently — at the cost of page-fault latency when data isn't in memory.",
